Command Line and Cron
*********************

.[perex]
A task can also be run by hand from the command line, and cron needs a command that takes over the work of a crashed process and reports what got stuck. `Butler\Cli` does both.


The Script
==========

A short script that creates the DI container and passes the arguments is all it takes:

```php
// bin/task.php
require __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php';

$container = (new App\Bootstrap)->bootConsoleApplication();
exit($container->getByType(Butler\Cli::class)->handle(array_slice($argv, 1)));
```

Without Nette, you build `Cli` yourself from the [runner |configuration#Without Nette]: `new Butler\Cli($runner)`.


Running a Task
==============

```shell
php bin/task.php run App.Tasks.ImportReleasesTask
```

The command records the run and processes the task right away. When another process is working on it at the moment, for example a request from a webhook, it waits until that process finishes, so the work is done when the command returns.

You write the name of the task with **dots instead of backslashes**. A shell eats unquoted backslashes, so `App\Tasks\ImportReleasesTask` would arrive in the script as `AppTasksImportReleasesTask`. Backslashes in quotes work too.

A `PayloadTask` needs a body. It takes it from a file, or with the character `-` from the standard input:

```shell
php bin/task.php run App.Tasks.PaypalTask ipn.txt
php bin/task.php run App.Tasks.PaypalTask - < ipn.txt
```

Without a file, `run` of a `PayloadTask` only processes the payloads already waiting in the queue and adds no new one.

A task that may run [only in production |tasks#Tasks That Run Only in Production] does not run locally, not even by hand.


Cron: drain
===========

```shell
php bin/task.php drain
```

`drain` processes all pending work of all tasks. It belongs in cron, typically every five minutes:

```
*/5 * * * * www-data php /var/www/example/bin/task.php drain
```

Why do you need it when webhooks trigger the tasks? Because the process working on a task can die: a restart of PHP-FPM, a timeout, running out of memory. The recorded work then waits for the next run. `drain` is that next run, the one that always comes, even when no webhook arrives for a long time.

At the same time it watches whether something got stuck:

```
App\Tasks\SponsorTask: failed
App\Tasks\SponsorTask: pending since 2026-11-18 03:05:33 UTC
App\Tasks\PaypalTask: 2 payloads rejected, see retry
```

It reports work pending for more than an hour and [rejected payloads |how-it-works#Rejected Payloads]. In that case it ends with code 1, so cron e-mails you the output.


Returning Rejected Payloads
===========================

A payload that failed repeatedly is set aside by Butler, so that it does not block the others. Once you have fixed the cause, you return it to the queue:

```shell
php bin/task.php retry App.Tasks.PaypalTask
```

The payloads return with the attempts reset and are processed right away.


Exit Codes
==========

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | everything ran |
| 1 | a task failed, something got stuck, or an error occurred (written to the error output) |
| 2 | wrong arguments, the usage is printed |

